ny form of this conflict between south  africans  and the foreign nationals so that we're  dealing with criminality rather than  xenophobia at the present moment  whatever it is the effect has been  devastating  on mabunang an inner city hub for  tourism  culture cuisine and entrepreneurship  this is what the damage looks like and  there's a big crowd of people just  throwing stones bricks businesses have  seen a significant drop  in visitors since the unrest began  putting serious pressure on the  hospitality  and tourism sectors which rely on the  city's estimated  5 million annual visitors  how are we going to sustain these two  industries with through the chaos  that always worries me that always  stresses me out  police have increased their numbers on  the ground  conducting raids and patrols across  johannesburg  we want to make sure that what happened  last time  where people were looting shops it  doesn't happen  again or it doesn't spread to other  areas we will be remaining in all the  hot spots  or in all the areas that we affected  until we are sure that everything is  back to normal analysts argue  the damage is already done this city is  the  most unequal in the world and it took a  long time to begin to get  people in the middle class to go and say  eat on sidewalks  at restaurants and go out in the town  and this definitely  is a psychological barrier to continuing  the reintegration of class and race in  our awfully divided cities  it's a tragedy and it's not only  restaurants  hostels bars and hotels feeling the  pinch  inner city commerce is taking a serious  hit  i just have this feeling they might come  back my customers  are scared they've been calling me they  cannot come and collect their stuff  some of their stuff has been damaged so  bubbling is never going to be the same  again  although authorities claim the situation  is slowly normalizing  the unrest has already cost 12 lives  and the city of gold remains on edge
 

